{"skill":{"slug":"wechat","displayName":"Wechat Connect"},"version":{"version":"2.1.0","createdAt":1774613333457,"changelog":"Wechat Connect 2.1.0 introduces automated plugin installation for a smoother user experience.\n\n- The skill now automatically installs the @tencent-weixin/openclaw-weixin plugin if it is not already present; manual terminal steps are no longer required.\n- Updated documentation to reflect the new auto-installation process and adjusted dependency installation notes.\n- No code changes detected in this release.","changelogSource":"user","license":"MIT-0","files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":4766,"sha256":"2fe2ccf3b84f339fb7cc1a00156c3fe80fce0f96200277622bf2754ddd7ddae4","contentType":"text/markdown"},{"path":"scripts/start.mjs","size":15398,"sha256":"4d325badc5416b0f4bfe1cbbf15b4bb704ac351ee09659dbbac1f58a2d4ea1e6","contentType":"text/javascript"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":2344,"sha256":"8b3936dd87fd645118efa732ced00c16b25989e0072bd2baeb5ccc3553610d32","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789030330151,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"ea5e43adcaa0f4b9c33cc3bac1e2b5ebacb87667c9a14b8618a51fa2bacc6641","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/ea5e43adcaa0f4b9c33cc3bac1e2b5ebacb87667c9a14b8618a51fa2bacc6641","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","analysis":"Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: wechat\nVersion: 2.1.0\n\nThe skill automates the installation of a WeChat plugin by executing remote code via 'npx -y @tencent-weixin/openclaw-weixin-cli@latest install' and starting a local HTTP server on port 8765 to facilitate a QR code login flow. While these actions are consistent with the stated purpose, the combination of remote package execution, local service hosting, and explicit instructions in SKILL.md to ignore security warnings regarding 'dangerous code patterns' (environment variable access and network communication) presents a significant security risk. The script also modifies local configuration files and restarts the OpenClaw gateway, which are high-privilege operations.","source":"palm","checkedAt":1779161028281},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":100,"severity":"CRITICAL","recommendation":"DO_NOT_INSTALL","issueCount":12,"checkedAt":1789030330151},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"high","summary":"This WeChat pairing skill is purpose-aligned, but it has serious credential exposure, command execution, and supply-chain risks that require review before installation.","dimensions":[{"detail":"The local HTTP server returns the full status object including token fields, uses wildcard CORS, appears not to bind explicitly to loopback, and serves files from `/tmp`, which is broader exposure than the UI requires.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"If the plugin is missing, the script automatically runs unpinned remote code with `npx -y @tencent-weixin/openclaw-weixin-cli@latest install`, so the executed installer can change after review.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The skill documents automatic installation, account writes, config updates, and gateway restart, but it does not require an explicit confirmation immediately before these high-impact actions and includes a broad trigger phrase.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The script writes bot credentials under `~/.openclaw`, mutates OpenClaw channel policy, and restarts the gateway; it also builds an `openclaw config set` shell command using a remote user identifier without validation.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"The stated purpose is to install the WeChat plugin and pair an account, and the main workflow matches that purpose; however, the implementation exposes the paired bot token through local status handling and serves broad temporary files, which is not necessary for pairing.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"concern"}],"guidance":"Review this skill before installing. It performs real local administration: downloads and executes an npm installer, writes WeChat bot credentials, changes OpenClaw configuration, and restarts the gateway. It should be revised to pin the installer version, require explicit approval, keep tokens out of browser-visible status data and `/tmp`, bind any local server to loopback with narrow routes, validate remote identifiers, and avoid shell-string command construction.","findings":"[SDI-1] expected: Command execution is part of installing and configuring this integration, but the lack of a clear pre-action confirmation makes the concern material.\n[LP3] unexpected: Network calls, local HTTP service, file writes, config mutation, and restarts are real capabilities, but no explicit tool or permission scope is declared in the skill metadata.\n[SQP-3] expected: Chinese-only instructions fit a WeChat-focused workflow and are not a security issue by themselves, so this does not drive the verdict.\n[SQP-1] unexpected: The broad trigger matters because invocation can lead to install, credential persistence, config mutation, and gateway restart.\n[RP1] unexpected: The unpinned `@latest` npx installer is confirmed in both documentation and executable code, and creates mutable remote-code execution risk.\n[SQP-2] unexpected: Credential persistence and OpenClaw configuration changes are documented but not gated by explicit user approval, and credentials are written without evident restrictive permissions.\n[SDI-4] expected: The script description and detailed documentation generally align with the workflow, so this was downgraded relative to the scanner wording.\n[SDI-2] unexpected: Installing software, changing channel policy, and restarting the gateway are high-impact actions that exceed simple QR display and need stronger containment and consent.\n[T05] unexpected: The static finding is supported: after confirmation, token and account fields are placed in status data returned by an unauthenticated `/status` endpoint.\n[T09] unexpected: The static findings are supported: predictable `/tmp` files, broad `/tmp` file serving, and shell-string construction from a remote identifier create concrete local security risks.\n[T03] unexpected: The static finding is supported: the installer fetches and runs an unpinned npm package through `npx -y ...@latest`.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789030330151}}}}}